---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { DMG, MIN_MACOS, REPO, VERSION } from "../data/release";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const home = new URL(import.meta.env.BASE_URL, Astro.site).href;
const description =
  "What carries over from Arc to Ardia, what doesn’t yet, and how to move your tabs, bookmarks and passwords over.";
---

<Base title="Coming from Arc? · Ardia" description={description}>
  <Header home={`${base}/`} />
  <main class="from-arc">
    <section class="intro" aria-labelledby="from-arc-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h1 class="poster" id="from-arc-title">Coming from Arc?</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Ardia is a free, open-source Chromium browser for Mac with an Arc-style sidebar. Here is what carries over,
          what doesn’t yet, and how to move your things across.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="keep-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="poster" id="keep-title">What you keep</h2>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>
            <strong>The sidebar.</strong> Tabs live in it, and the address bar can sit there too, so the page runs to
            the top of the window.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Pinned tabs.</strong> Pinned as tiles or as rows, and they belong to the profile.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Split view.</strong> Drag a tab onto the page. Up to three panes, side by side or stacked.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Little Arc.</strong> ⌥⌘N opens a small window with one page and no sidebar.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Tab groups.</strong> Drop a tab on another tab and they become a group.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The command bar.</strong> ⌘L finds tabs, history and bookmarks, does sums, and runs shortcuts like
            doc.new.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Profiles and themes.</strong> Each profile keeps its own logins, history, extensions and colour.
            Arc’s theme can be imported.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Live folders.</strong> GitHub pull requests and documents, shown in the sidebar. They need your own
            token or OAuth app to connect.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="differs-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="poster" id="differs-title">What differs</h2>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>
            <strong>No Arc spaces yet.</strong> Ardia uses profiles for the same job: a profile is a separate set of
            logins, history and extensions, switched by swiping the sidebar.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No Boosts.</strong> Ardia doesn’t restyle sites for you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Extensions are reinstalled, not copied.</strong> The Chrome Web Store works, and its button says
            Add to Ardia. Arc’s extensions show up in the import as a list to reinstall.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No Netflix yet.</strong> Protected video needs a Widevine license that Google hasn’t granted.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Passkeys in iCloud Keychain are not yet.</strong> Touch ID works. Apple has to approve browsers
            before iCloud Keychain passkeys work.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sync is a folder, not an account.</strong> It’s end-to-end encrypted and goes through a folder you
            pick, by default in your own iCloud Drive. There is no Ardia account and no server of ours.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Mac only, Apple Silicon only.</strong> It needs {MIN_MACOS} or later. No Intel Macs.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No AI features.</strong> That’s on purpose.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="move-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="poster" id="move-title">How to move over</h2>

        <div class="step">
          <h3>1. Download Ardia</h3>
          <p>
            Version {VERSION}, notarized by Apple. Needs {MIN_MACOS} or later on Apple Silicon.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a
              class="btn"
              href={DMG}
              data-download
              data-track="download_clicked"
              data-track-version={VERSION}
              data-track-location="from_arc"
            >Download for Mac</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
          <h3>2. Import from Arc</h3>
          <p>
            If Arc is still on this Mac, open Ardia’s menu and choose Import from Another Browser…, then pick Arc.
            You can bring bookmarks, history, passwords, cookies, addresses and cards, tabs, pinned tabs, favorites
            and spaces.
          </p>
          <p>
            Arc’s spaces come in as pinned tabs and an Imported group in the profile you choose. Their pinned folders
            become a bookmarks folder named for the space, and a space’s theme becomes that profile’s theme. Passwords, cookies and cards
            are encrypted, so macOS asks you to unlock Arc’s keychain item first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="step">
          <h3>3. Or move by hand</h3>
          <p>
            If Arc isn’t on this Mac, import a bookmarks HTML file from the import window, or from Import Bookmarks…
            on the bookmarks page. Import a passwords CSV from Settings › Passwords.
          </p>
          <p>
            Extensions aren’t imported. Reinstall them from the Chrome Web Store, and each one’s button says Add to
            Ardia.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="fine">
          Questions or something missing? <a class="link" href={`${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">Open an issue on GitHub</a>.
          Ardia isn’t affiliated with Arc or The Browser Company.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .from-arc {
    padding-bottom: var(--section);
  }
  .intro {
    padding: clamp(72px, 9vw, 140px) 0 clamp(40px, 5vw, 72px);
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 1.2rem + 4.6vw, 6rem);
    max-width: 14ch;
  }
  .lede {
    margin-top: 28px;
    max-width: 38em;
    font-size: var(--t-lede);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .block {
    padding: clamp(40px, 5vw, 72px) 0;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 3vw, 4rem);
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }
  .list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: 44em;
  }
  .list li {
    padding: 18px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
  }
  .list strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 620;
  }
  .step {
    max-width: 44em;
    padding: 22px 0 6px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
  h3 {
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  .step p {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 18px;
    font-family: var(--poster);
    font-weight: 740;
    font-stretch: 85%;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    text-decoration: none;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--paper);
    border-radius: 3px;
  }
  .btn:hover {
    background: var(--accent);
  }
  .fine {
    margin-top: 40px;
    max-width: 44em;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .fine a {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
